Engine subsystems should reuse the cached `GpuDevice` wrapper returned by `getGpuDevice()`. The wrapper owns the native `GPUDevice`, its queue, granted features, limits, and device-loss callbacks. Resources created by one native device cannot be used by another, so pass the shared wrapper or native device into each subsystem that needs GPU access.

**Example: Acquire the shared engine device**
```js
import { getGpuDevice } from '/engine/core/gpu/GpuDevice.js';

const gpu = await getGpuDevice({
  label: 'MyEngineApp',
  adapterOptions: { powerPreference: 'high-performance' },
  requiredFeatures: []
});

const device = gpu.getDevice();
const queue = gpu.getQueue();
```

Request a feature only when the application cannot run without it. `GpuDevice.create()` automatically requests the engine's supported optional feature set, while `gpu.getCapabilities().features` reports the features actually granted to the device.

**Feature and limit checks**
```js
const { features, limits } = gpu.getCapabilities();

if (features.has('timestamp-query')) {
  console.info('GPU timestamp queries are available');
}

const requestedBytes = 64 * 1024 * 1024;
if (requestedBytes > limits.maxBufferSize) {
  throw new RangeError('Requested buffer exceeds maxBufferSize');
}
```

**Device loss**
```js
gpu.onDeviceLost((info) => {
  console.error('WebGPU device lost:', info.reason, info.message);
  // Stop work and re-bootstrap the application so every GPU resource is rebuilt.
});
```

**Gotchas:**
- **First acquisition defines requirements:** `getGpuDevice()` caches the wrapper. Supply mandatory features and limits on the first call.
- **Native access:** use `gpu.getDevice()` and `gpu.getQueue()` when a subsystem needs WebGPU primitives.
- **Recovery:** this module reports device loss but does not expose an in-place `recreate()` API. Re-bootstrap all device-owned resources together.
- **Limits:** check the granted `gpu.getCapabilities().limits` before large allocations.

(Source: `engine/core/gpu/GpuDevice.js`)

**See also:** [GPU Device Sharing](/concepts/gpu-device-sharing.md) · [Virtual GPU](/engine/vgpu.md) · [GpuFormats](/engine/core/gpu/GpuFormats.md)

OS apps ship as signed `.prpkg` bundles (V2: signed + encrypted + merkle + cert). A developer (self-signed) key verifies as the **community / unofficial** tier; a root-issued cert promotes it to **trusted**, and capability grants scale with the tier.

**Example: Loading a package with trust check**
```js
import { PackageLoader } from 'webgpu-os/packages/PackageLoader.js';

const pkg = await PackageLoader.load('./my-app.prpkg', {
  requiredTier: 'community',  // or 'trusted' for stricter caps
  capabilities: ['storage', 'network']
});

// Trust tier determines what APIs are available
if (pkg.trustTier === 'trusted') {
  pkg.grantCapability('raw-gpu');
}
```

**See also:** [Security & Trust Model](/concepts/security-model.md) · [WebGPU OS Architecture](/webgpu-os/architecture.md)

AGI runs an **observation → brain → action → reward** loop each tick: observations are gathered from the world, a brain maps them to actions, the rig applies motion, and rewards shape the next update.

**Example: Minimal training loop**
```js
import { Agent } from 'agi/core/Agent.js';
import { World } from 'agi/env/World.js';

const world = new World();
const agent = new Agent({
  brain: 'ppo',
  observationSpace: world.observationSpace,
  actionSpace: world.actionSpace
});

for (let step = 0; step < 10000; step++) {
  const obs = world.getObservation();      // Gather observations
  const action = agent.act(obs);            // Brain decides action
  const { reward, done } = world.step(action); // Apply action, get reward
  agent.learn(obs, action, reward);         // Update from experience

  if (done) world.reset();
}
```

**See also:** [AGI Training Guide](/agi/training-guide.md) · [AGI Architecture](/agi/architecture.md)

Plauna apps and widgets follow a **mount → update → unmount** lifecycle; services (storage, command bus, theming) are *injected* rather than imported, so the same widget runs in the editor, the OS shell, and standalone.

**Example: Widget with full lifecycle**
```js
import { Widget } from 'plauna/core/Widget.js';
import { useStorage, useCommands } from 'plauna/hooks.js';

class CounterWidget extends Widget {
  mount(container, services) {
    // Inject dependencies
    this.storage = useStorage(services);
    this.commands = useCommands(services);

    this.el = document.createElement('div');
    this.count = this.storage.get('count', 0);
    this.render();
    container.appendChild(this.el);

    // Register commands
    this.commands.register('counter:reset', () => {
      this.count = 0;
      this.render();
    });
  }

  update(props) {
    if (props.increment) this.count++;
    this.render();
  }

  render() {
    this.el.textContent = `Count: ${this.count}`;
    this.storage.set('count', this.count);
  }

  unmount() {
    this.commands.unregister('counter:reset');
    this.el.remove();
  }
}
```

**See also:** [Plauna Architecture](/plauna/architecture.md) · [Plauna Overview](/plauna/overview.md)

The Entity Component System (ECS) separates data (Components) from behavior (Systems). Entities are lightweight IDs that group components. Systems query entities by component type and process them in bulk, enabling cache-friendly iteration and parallel execution.

**Example: Creating and querying entities**
```js
import { World } from 'engine/ecs/World.js';
import { Component } from 'engine/ecs/Component.js';
import { System } from 'engine/ecs/System.js';

// Define component types
class Position extends Component { x = 0; y = 0; z = 0; }
class Velocity extends Component { x = 0; y = 0; z = 0; }
class Health extends Component { current = 100; max = 100; }

// Create world and entities
const world = new World();
const player = world.createEntity();
player.add(new Position(0, 0, 0));
player.add(new Velocity(1, 0, 0));
player.add(new Health(100, 100));
```

**Advanced: Complex queries**
```js
// Query entities with Position AND Velocity but NOT Static
class MovementSystem extends System {
  query = [Position, Velocity];
  exclude = ['Static'];  // Skip immovable objects

  update(entities, dt) {
    for (const entity of entities) {
      const pos = entity.get(Position);
      const vel = entity.get(Velocity);
      pos.x += vel.x * dt;
      pos.y += vel.y * dt;
      pos.z += vel.z * dt;
    }
  }
}

// Query with OR conditions
const damaged = world.query([Health], {
  filter: (e) => e.get(Health).current < e.get(Health).max
});
```

**Advanced: System groups and ordering**
```js
// Systems run in group order, within group by priority
world.addSystem(new InputSystem(), { group: 'input', priority: 0 });
world.addSystem(new AISystem(), { group: 'ai', priority: 0 });
world.addSystem(new MovementSystem(), { group: 'physics', priority: 0 });
world.addSystem(new CollisionSystem(), { group: 'physics', priority: 1 });
world.addSystem(new RenderSystem(), { group: 'render', priority: 0 });

// Groups run: input → ai → physics → render
world.update(0.016);
```

**Advanced: Component events**
```js
// React to component add/remove
world.onComponentAdded(Health, (entity, health) => {
  console.log('Entity', entity.id, 'health set to', health.max);
});

world.onComponentRemoved(Health, (entity) => {
  console.log('Entity', entity.id, 'died (health removed)');
});
```

**Gotchas:**
- **Adding components during iteration**: Safe, but new entities won't appear until next frame.
- **Removing entities**: Mark for deletion, processed at end of frame to avoid iterator invalidation.
- **Singleton components**: Use `world.getResource(Singleton)` for global state like Time, Input.

**See also:** [ECS v2](/engine/ecs.md) · [Engine Architecture](/engine/architecture.md)

The audio system uses a node-based graph architecture. AudioNodes connect to form processing chains: sources (oscillator, buffer) → effects (filter, delay, reverb) → destination (speakers). All processing happens on the audio thread via the Web Audio API with automatic sample rate conversion.

**Example: Creating a sound effect chain**
```js
import { AudioContext } from 'engine/audio/AudioContext.js';
import { OscillatorNode } from 'engine/audio/OscillatorNode.js';
import { GainNode } from 'engine/audio/GainNode.js';
import { BiquadFilterNode } from 'engine/audio/BiquadFilterNode.js';

const ctx = new AudioContext();

// Build chain: oscillator → filter → gain → speakers
const osc = new OscillatorNode(ctx, { type: 'sawtooth', frequency: 440 });
const filter = new BiquadFilterNode(ctx, { type: 'lowpass', frequency: 800 });
const gain = new GainNode(ctx, { gain: 0.5 });

osc.connect(filter);
filter.connect(gain);
gain.connect(ctx.destination);

// Trigger sound with envelope
osc.start();
gain.gain.setTargetAtTime(0, ctx.currentTime + 0.1, 0.1);  // Decay
osc.stop(ctx.currentTime + 2);
```

**Advanced: Loading and playing audio assets**
```js
import { AudioBufferLoader } from 'engine/audio/AudioBufferLoader.js';
import { AudioBufferSourceNode } from 'engine/audio/AudioBufferSourceNode.js';

// Load audio file
const loader = new AudioBufferLoader(ctx);
const buffer = await loader.load('/sounds/explosion.wav');

// Play with variations
function playExplosion(intensity = 1.0) {
  const source = new AudioBufferSourceNode(ctx, { buffer });
  const gain = new GainNode(ctx, { gain: intensity });
  const filter = new BiquadFilterNode(ctx, {
    type: 'lowpass',
    frequency: 1000 + Math.random() * 2000
  });

  source.playbackRate.value = 0.9 + Math.random() * 0.2;
  source.connect(filter);
  filter.connect(gain);
  gain.connect(ctx.destination);

  source.start();
}

// Pool voices for polyphony
const pool = new AudioVoicePool(ctx, { maxVoices: 16 });
pool.play(buffer, { pitch: 1.0, volume: 0.8 });
```

**Advanced: 3D spatial audio**
```js
import { PannerNode } from 'engine/audio/PannerNode.js';

// Create spatial sound for an entity
const entityPos = [10, 0, 5];
const panner = new PannerNode(ctx, {
  positionX: entityPos[0],
  positionY: entityPos[1],
  positionZ: entityPos[2],
  refDistance: 5,     // Audible within 5 units
  maxDistance: 50,    // Silent beyond 50 units
  rolloffFactor: 1,   // Linear falloff
  panningModel: 'HRTF'  // Head-related transfer function for realism
});

// Connect spatial source to master
source.connect(panner);
panner.connect(ctx.destination);

// Update position each frame
function updateAudio() {
  panner.positionX.value = entity.position[0];
  panner.positionY.value = entity.position[1];
  panner.positionZ.value = entity.position[2];
}
```

**Advanced: Mixing and buses**
```js
// Create mix buses for layering
const sfxBus = new GainNode(ctx, { gain: 0.8 });
const musicBus = new GainNode(ctx, { gain: 0.5 });
const voiceBus = new GainNode(ctx, { gain: 1.0 });

// Add compression/limiting to master
const compressor = new DynamicsCompressorNode(ctx, {
  threshold: -24,
  knee: 30,
  ratio: 12,
  attack: 0.003,
  release: 0.25
});

// Route to master
sfxBus.connect(compressor);
musicBus.connect(compressor);
voiceBus.connect(compressor);
compressor.connect(ctx.destination);

// Duck music when voice plays
voiceBus.connect(new GainNode(ctx)).gain.setValueAtTime(0.3, ctx.currentTime);
```

**Gotchas:**
- **AudioContext state**: Browsers block audio until user interaction. Call `await ctx.resume()` after a click.
- **Memory**: `AudioBuffer` holds raw PCM — unload large files when not needed.
- **Scheduling**: Use `start(time)` for precise rhythm; `currentTime` drifts vs `performance.now()`.

**See also:** [Audio](/engine/audio.md) · [Engine Overview](/engine/overview.md)

The physics system provides rigid body dynamics, collision detection, and raycasting. Bodies have shapes (box, sphere, mesh), mass properties, and constraints. The GPU physics engine accelerates broad-phase collision detection and particle systems.

**Example: Creating physics bodies**
```js
import { PhysicsWorld } from 'engine/physics/PhysicsWorld.js';
import { RigidBody } from 'engine/physics/RigidBody.js';
import { BoxShape, SphereShape } from 'engine/physics/Shapes.js';

const world = new PhysicsWorld({ gravity: [0, -9.8, 0] });

// Static ground
const ground = new RigidBody({
  type: 'static',
  shape: new BoxShape(10, 1, 10),
  position: [0, -0.5, 0]
});
world.addBody(ground);

// Dynamic box
const box = new RigidBody({
  type: 'dynamic',
  mass: 1,
  shape: new BoxShape(1, 1, 1),
  position: [0, 5, 0],
  restitution: 0.5  // Bounciness
});
world.addBody(box);
```

**Advanced: Collision detection and raycasting**
```js
// Raycast to find what the player is looking at
const hit = world.raycast(camera.position, camera.forward, 100);
if (hit) {
  console.log('Hit entity:', hit.body.entityId);
  console.log('Hit point:', hit.point);
  console.log('Hit normal:', hit.normal);

  // Apply impulse at hit point
  hit.body.applyImpulse([0, 10, 0], hit.point);
}

// Shapecast (swept sphere) for character movement
const sweep = world.shapecast(
  player.position,           // Start
  desiredPosition,           // End
  new SphereShape(0.5),    // Player capsule radius
  { ignore: player.body }  // Don't collide with self
);
if (sweep.hit) {
  // Slide along wall
  player.position = sweep.position;
}
```

**Advanced: Constraints and joints**
```js
import { HingeConstraint, FixedConstraint } from 'engine/physics/Constraints.js';

// Door hinge
const doorHinge = new HingeConstraint({
  bodyA: doorFrame,
  bodyB: door,
  pivot: [0, 1, 0],      // Hinge location
  axis: [0, 1, 0],       // Hinge axis (Y up)
  limits: [-90, 90]      // Open angle range in degrees
});
world.addConstraint(doorHinge);

// Ragdoll joint (ball-and-socket)
const shoulder = new FixedConstraint({
  bodyA: torso,
  bodyB: arm,
  pivotA: [0.5, 1.5, 0],  // Shoulder position on torso
  pivotB: [0, 0, 0]       // Shoulder position on arm
});
world.addConstraint(shoulder);
```

**Advanced: Continuous collision detection (CCD)**
```js
// Fast-moving objects need CCD to not tunnel through walls
const projectile = new RigidBody({
  type: 'dynamic',
  shape: new SphereShape(0.1),
  mass: 0.1,
  ccd: { enabled: true, sweptSphereRadius: 0.1 },
  linearDamping: 0  // No air resistance for bullets
});
projectile.velocity = [0, 0, 100];  // Very fast
world.addBody(projectile);
```

**Gotchas:**
- **Units**: Physics uses meters, kilograms, seconds. A 1-unit cube is 1 meter.
- **Time step**: Fixed time step (1/60) gives stable simulation; variable dt causes explosions.
- **Collision layers**: Use collision masks to filter what collides (player vs enemy, bullet vs world).
- **Sleeping**: At-rest bodies sleep to save CPU — wake them before applying forces.

**See also:** [Physics](/engine/physics.md) · [GPU Physics Engine](/engine/gpu-physics.md)

The input system provides unified keyboard, mouse, gamepad, and touch handling with action mapping. Actions abstract physical inputs (e.g., 'jump' can be Space, A button, or tap) and support chorded inputs (combinations) and sequences.

**Example: Action-based input mapping**
```js
import { InputManager } from 'engine/core/input/InputManager.js';
import { Keyboard } from 'engine/core/input/Keyboard.js';
import { Gamepad } from 'engine/core/input/Gamepad.js';

const input = new InputManager();

// Map actions to inputs
input.mapAction('jump', {
  keyboard: 'Space',
  gamepad: 'A',
  touch: 'tap'
});

input.mapAction('sprint', {
  keyboard: 'ShiftLeft',
  gamepad: 'LeftStickPress'
});

// Chord: both must be pressed
input.mapAction('crouch-jump', {
  chord: ['Control', 'Space']
});

// In game loop
input.update();
if (input.isActionPressed('jump')) {
  player.jump();
}
if (input.isActionHeld('sprint')) {
  player.speed *= 2;
}
```

**See also:** [Engine Stack Usage](/guides/engine-stack-usage.md)

The voxel system provides chunked 3D grids with fast access, mesh generation, and level-of-detail (LOD). Chunks are 32³ by default and stream in/out based on camera position. The mesher generates greedy or marching-cubes geometry depending on material.

**Example: Creating and modifying a voxel world**
```js
import { VoxelWorld } from 'engine/voxel/VoxelWorld.js';
import { VoxelMesher } from 'engine/voxel/VoxelMesher.js';

const world = new VoxelWorld({
  chunkSize: 32,
  loadDistance: 8  // chunks
});

// Set voxel at world position
world.setVoxel(10, 5, 10, {
  type: 'grass',
  color: [0.2, 0.7, 0.1]
});

world.setVoxel(10, 4, 10, { type: 'dirt' });

// Generate mesh for rendering
const mesher = new VoxelMesher(world);
const geometry = mesher.generateChunk(0, 0, 0);

// Raycast to find clicked voxel
const hit = world.raycast(rayOrigin, rayDir, 100);
if (hit) {
  world.setVoxel(hit.x, hit.y, hit.z, null);  // Remove
}
```

**See also:** [Engine Overview](/engine/overview.md)

The world system manages scene graphs, asset streaming, and level transitions. Scenes are entity hierarchies with spatial indexing (BVH) for fast culling and raycasts. Worlds stream assets asynchronously with priority based on distance and visibility.

**Example: Scene graph and streaming**
```js
import { World } from 'engine/world/World.js';
import { Scene } from 'engine/scenes/Scene.js';
import { Entity } from 'engine/ecs/Entity.js';

const world = new World();
const scene = new Scene();
world.addScene(scene);

// Create hierarchy
const player = new Entity('Player');
const camera = new Entity('Camera');
const weapon = new Entity('Weapon');

player.addChild(camera);
camera.addChild(weapon);  // Weapon follows camera

scene.add(player);

// Stream in a level
await world.streamLevel('level1', {
  priority: 'high',
  bounds: player.boundingBox,
  onProgress: (pct) => console.log(`Loading: ${pct}%`)
});

// Spatial query: find entities in radius
const nearby = scene.querySphere(player.position, 50);
```

**See also:** [Engine Overview](/engine/overview.md)

The math library provides vector, matrix, quaternion, and utility operations. All math functions follow consistent patterns: **pure functions** return new values, **`*Into` variants** write to an output parameter (zero allocation), and **out parameters come first**. Matrices are column-major for WebGPU compatibility.

**Example: Common math operations**
```js
import { vec3, vec3Add, vec3Scale, vec3Normalize } from 'engine/core/math/MathVec3.js';
import { mat4, mat4Multiply, mat4Perspective, mat4LookAt } from 'engine/core/math/MathMat4.js';
import { quat, quatMultiply, quatFromEuler } from 'engine/core/math/MathQuat.js';

// Vectors
const pos = vec3(1, 2, 3);
const dir = vec3Normalize(vec3(1, 0, 0));

// Matrix: projection * view * model
const proj = mat4Perspective(60 * Math.PI/180, 16/9, 0.1, 1000);
const view = mat4LookAt([0, 5, 10], [0, 0, 0], [0, 1, 0]);
const model = mat4();  // Identity

// Into variant for hot loops (no allocation)
const mvp = mat4();
mat4Multiply(mvp, view, model);  // mvp = view * model
mat4Multiply(mvp, proj, mvp);    // mvp = proj * view * model

// Quaternion rotation
const rot = quatFromEuler(0, Math.PI/4, 0);  // 45° Y rotation
```

**See also:** [Math Library](/engine/math.md)

The OS uses a capability-gated command bus for inter-app and system communication. Apps register command handlers; the kernel routes commands based on capabilities. Commands can be local (same app), cross-app (with permission), or system-level (elevated).

**Example: Command registration and invocation**
```js
import { CommandBus } from 'webgpu-os/kernel/CommandBus.js';
import { Capability } from 'webgpu-os/kernel/Capability.js';

const bus = new CommandBus();

// Register a handler (requires 'ui' capability)
bus.register('myapp:openFile', async (path) => {
  const content = await storage.read(path);
  return { content, size: content.length };
}, { capability: 'storage' });

// Invoke locally
const result = await bus.invoke('myapp:openFile', '/docs/readme.md');

// Cross-app invocation (requires 'interop' capability)
const otherApp = bus.proxy('otherapp');
const data = await otherApp.invoke('otherapp:compute', input);

// Broadcast to all listening apps
bus.broadcast('system:themeChanged', { theme: 'dark' });
```

**See also:** [WebGPU OS Architecture](/webgpu-os/architecture.md) · [Security Model](/concepts/security-model.md)

The editor supports extensions via the same Plauna component system as apps. Extensions can add panels, tools, menu items, and hooks into the engine. They run with elevated 'editor' capability granting access to the scene graph, asset database, and build pipeline.

**Example: Editor extension adding a custom panel**
```js
import { Extension } from 'editor/core/Extension.js';
import { Panel } from 'editor/ui/Panel.js';

class TerrainEditor extends Extension {
  id = 'terrain-editor';
  capabilities = ['editor', 'scene'];

  activate(context) {
    // Add menu item
    context.menus.add('Tools/Terrain', () => this.open());

    // Register tool
    context.tools.register('terrain', {
      icon: 'mountain',
      onSelect: (entity) => this.edit(entity)
    });
  }

  open() {
    this.panel = new Panel({
      title: 'Terrain Editor',
      content: this.buildUI()
    });
    context.workspace.addPanel(this.panel);
  }

  edit(entity) {
    const terrain = entity.get(TerrainComponent);
    this.panel.bind(terrain);
  }
}

editor.extensions.register(TerrainEditor);
```

**See also:** [Editor Overview](/editor/overview.md)
